            <ArticleTitle>Coronary sinus compression as a sign of cardiac tamponade.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>455-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary perforation and resultant cardiac tamponade are well-known but rare complications of percutaneous coronary interventions. We present a case that demonstrates coronary sinus compression caused by increasing pericardial pressure as a new sign of impending cardiac tamponade. This previously unreported angiographic sign preceded hemodynamic, symptomatic, and echocardiographic evidence of tamponade.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
